--TEST--
Core\IO::within is the path sink's launderer — a tainted path may be declared as the plain `string` it answers, and every other member of the class then accepts that answer where the tainted value itself is refused — ADR 0024 § 3, ADR 0088 § 1
--FILE--
<?nvs
// ADR 0024 § 3 gives exactly one way out of `tainted`: a narrow `Core` member
// whose return type is the plain type and whose contract names the one sink it
// is safe for. `within` names the path sink -- which, by ADR 0088 § 1's table,
// is every path parameter of this class, because `..` and the separators direct
// the resolver. The refusal itself is
// tests/conformance/reject/a-core-io-path-refuses-a-tainted-argument.nvst; what
// this file asserts is the route past it.
string $base = Core\IO::temporaryDir();
string $note = Core\Path::join($base, "note.txt");
Core\IO::write($note, "laundered\n");

// The untrusted half. Declaring the answer a plain `string` *is* the assertion:
// a contagious row could not be spelled this way, and a sink would not have
// taken the argument at all.
tainted string $wanted = "note.txt";
string $safe = Core\IO::within($base, $wanted);

// And the sinks the row launders for take that answer. What earns the
// laundering is that containment was proved rather than assumed: the value
// reaching `read` is a path the resolver has already agreed with.
echo Core\IO::read($safe);
int $size = Core\IO::size($safe) as int;
echo "size: ", $size as string, "\n";

Core\IO::remove($safe);
Core\IO::removeDir($base);
echo "gone: ", Core\IO::exists($base) ? "y" : "n", "\n";
--FILE nvs.toml--
[capabilities.fs]
read = true
write = true
--EXPECT--
laundered
size: 10
gone: n
